

//
// Rue Programming Language
//
// Syntax Upgrades: May 23, 2026
// "Method 3" Transition.
//
// This files explores specific
// syntax differences between
// the current "old" syntax,
// and the new, transformative
// syntax that is actively being
// implemented into the Rue
// programming language.
//

// First, let's create a simple
// component: Square Text
// ...
// This component will return
// a simple UI Element that
// contains the inputted text.

// Old Syntax
component SquareText(text) {
    return new UIElement({
        padding: 2.4rem
        background: black
        color: white
        content: text
    })
}

// New Syntax
component SquareText(text) {
    new UIElement(text)
        padding: 2.4rem
        background: black
        color: white
}

// We have restructured how RueUI
// object instantiations are made
// ...
// In this new syntax, new elements
// recieve their primary value as
// the constructor call, and get
// passed attributes (config)
// as succeeding, indented lines
// following the inital call. This
// syntax change does introduce
// intendatation rules to Rue.
// ...
// Additionally, components are
// starting to further develop
// their identity in Rue. Instead
// of just being a reformatted
// function, components entire 
// bodies now define their return
// values, removing the need to
// define a "return" value.
// ... 
// All defined components
// (including plain strings)
// in a component's body will
// be returned as an array that
// will later be handled via
// RueUI HTML conversions.

//
// --- --- ---
//

// Next, let's define small
// interfaces that displays
// simple text to the screen.

// Old Syntax
Interface {
    new UIElement({
        content: "Hi"
        font-size: 1.2rem
        font-weight: 600
    })
    // new Component({ 
    //      value,
    //      ...config 
    // })
}

// New Syntax
Interface {
    new UIElement("Hi")
        font-size: 1.2rem
        font-weight: 600
    // new Component(value)
    //      ...config
}

// Notice the changes in how
// object instantiations are made
// ...
// RueUI changes should not be
// necessary to implement these
// syntax changes. I have just created
// a new class that is exported from
// interface.ts named "Component"
// ...
// It takes two inputs, content and
// config. It is possible to modify the
// Rue syntax to follow the default format
// for RueUI element types: (main val, config)
// ...
// For example new VStack takes 
// (array of elements, config))
// ...
// The default text display component in Rue
// is now the new Component(val) ... a change
// that is needed is the ability to detect
// succeeding attribute lines, and to apply them
// to object instantiation config value.